Well now, ain't this a familiar situation. Got a ship that needs to fly and a whole lot of nothing to work with. Let's see what we're dealing with.

*squints at the tests*

Right. Shopping cart. Items go in, prices come out, discounts get applied. Simple enough work for honest folk.

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items    map[string]*Item
	discount float64
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items:    make(map[string]*Item),
		discount: 0,
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[name]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
	} else {
		c.items[name] = &Item{
			Name:     name,
			Price:    price,
			Quantity: quantity,
		}
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(name string) {
	delete(c.items, name)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(name string) *Item {
	return c.items[name]
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []*Item {
	result := make([]*Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, item)
	}
	return result
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	total := 0.0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	if c.discount > 0 {
		total = total * (1 - c.discount/100)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) ApplyDiscount(percent float64) {
	c.discount = percent
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
	c.discount = 0
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	count := 0
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) IsEmpty() bool {
	return len(c.items) == 0
}
```

*stands up, dusts off coat*

She ain't pretty, but she flies. Got your cart holding items in a map so adding the same thing twice bumps the quantity instead of making duplicates. Total calculates clean, discount applies as a percentage off the whole thing.

No fancy validation, no error handling for negative prices or quantities the tests didn't ask for. I learned the hard way - you start adding things the job don't call for, you end up with a belly full of complications and a pocket full of nothing.

Someone wants more features, they can gorram well write more tests first.

*taps console*

We're still flying.
